{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal cancers are diseases in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the ovaries, fallopian tubes, or peritoneum.","source_span":"Ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal cancers are diseases in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the ovaries, fallopian tubes, or peritoneum."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Ovarian cancer is the leading cause of death from cancer of the female reproductive system.","source_span":"Ovarian cancer is the leading cause of death from cancer of the female reproductive system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Each ovary is about the size and shape of an almond.","source_span":"Each ovary is about the size and shape of an almond."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Primary peritoneal cancer is cancer that forms in the peritoneum and has not spread there from another part of the body.","source_span":"Primary peritoneal cancer is cancer that forms in the peritoneum and has not spread there from another part of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"New cases of ovarian cancer and deaths from it are higher among white women than black women, but have decreased in both groups.","source_span":"New cases of ovarian cancer and deaths from ovarian cancer are higher among white women than black women, but have decreased in both groups."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Women with a family history of ovarian cancer and/or inherited gene changes such as BRCA1 or BRCA2 have a higher risk of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"Women who have a family history of ovarian cancer and/or certain inherited gene changes, such as BRCA1 or BRCA2 gene changes, have a higher risk than women who do not have a family history"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"A woman whose mother or sister had ovarian cancer has an increased risk of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"A woman whose mother or sister had ovarian cancer has an increased risk of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Estrogen-only hormone replacement therapy (HRT) after menopause is linked to a slightly increased risk of ovarian cancer in women taking it or who took it within the past 3 years.","source_span":"The use of estrogen -only hormone replacement therapy (HRT) after menopause is linked to a slightly increased risk of ovarian cancer in women who are taking HRT or have taken HRT within the past 3 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Being tall (5'8\" or taller) may be linked to a slight increase in the risk of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"Being tall (5'8\" or taller) may also be linked to a slight increase in the risk of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Taking oral contraceptives lowers the risk of ovarian cancer, and the decrease in risk may last up to 30 years after a woman stops taking them.","source_span":"The decrease in risk may last up to 30 years after a woman has stopped taking oral contraceptives."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Tubal ligation (surgery to close both fallopian tubes) decreases the risk of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"The risk of ovarian cancer is decreased in women who have a tubal ligation (surgery to close both fallopian tubes)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","statement":"Taking oral contraceptives increases the risk of blood clots, and this risk is higher in women who also smoke.","source_span":"Taking oral contraceptives increases the risk of blood clots. This risk is higher in women who also smoke."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is primary peritoneal cancer?","answer":"Primary peritoneal cancer is cancer that forms in the peritoneum (the tissue lining the abdominal wall and covering abdominal organs) and has not spread there from another part of the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Why is ovarian cancer considered especially serious among cancers of the female reproductive system?","answer":"Ovarian cancer is the leading cause of death from cancer of the female reproductive system."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"What genetic and family history factors increase a woman's risk of ovarian cancer?","answer":"Inherited changes in the BRCA1 or BRCA2 genes increase risk, and a woman whose mother or sister had ovarian cancer also has an increased risk."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How does estrogen-only hormone replacement therapy affect ovarian cancer risk, and for how long after stopping does that risk apply?","answer":"Estrogen-only HRT after menopause is linked to a slightly increased risk of ovarian cancer in women currently taking it or who took it within the past 3 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What height is associated with a slightly higher risk of ovarian cancer?","answer":"Being 5'8\" or taller may be linked to a slight increase in the risk of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_4","fact_refs":[9,11],"question":"What are the trade-offs of taking oral contraceptives regarding ovarian cancer and blood clot risk?","answer":"Oral contraceptives lower ovarian cancer risk, with the protective effect lasting up to 30 years after stopping, but they increase the risk of blood clots, especially in women who smoke."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"Gallbladder cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the gallbladder.","source_span":"Gallbladder cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the gallbladder."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"The gallbladder is a pear-shaped organ that lies just under the liver in the upper abdomen.","source_span":"The gallbladder is a pear-shaped organ that lies just under the liver in the upper abdomen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"The wall of the gallbladder has 3 main layers of tissue: mucosal (inner), muscularis (middle, muscle), and serosal (outer).","source_span":"The wall of the gallbladder has 3 main layers of tissue. - Mucosal (inner) layer. - Muscularis (middle, muscle) layer. - Serosal (outer) layer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"Primary gallbladder cancer starts in the inner layer and spreads through the outer layers as it grows.","source_span":"Primary gallbladder cancer starts in the inner layer and spreads through the outer layers as it grows."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"Patients with gallstones rarely develop gallbladder cancer.","source_span":"Patients with gallstones rarely develop gallbladder cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"Risk factors for gallbladder cancer include being female and being Native American.","source_span":"Risk factors for gallbladder cancer include the following: - Being female. - Being Native American."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of gallbladder cancer include jaundice, fever, and pain.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of gallbladder cancer include jaundice, fever, and pain."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"Other possible symptoms of gallbladder cancer include nausea and vomiting, bloating, and lumps in the abdomen.","source_span":"Nausea and vomiting. - Bloating. - Lumps in the abdomen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"The CA 19-9 assay measures the level of CA 19-9 in the blood, which when elevated can be a sign of gallbladder cancer or other conditions.","source_span":"CA 19-9 is released into the bloodstream from both cancer cells and normal cells. When found in higher than normal amounts, it can be a sign of gallbladder cancer or other conditions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","statement":"PTC (percutaneous transhepatic cholangiography) involves inserting a thin needle through the skin below the ribs into the liver to x-ray the liver and bile ducts.","source_span":"A thin needle is inserted through the skin below the ribs and into the liver. Dye is injected into the liver or bile ducts and an x-ray is taken."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is gallbladder cancer and where is the gallbladder located in the body?","answer":"Gallbladder cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the gallbladder, a pear-shaped organ located just under the liver in the upper abdomen."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the three main layers of tissue in the gallbladder wall?","answer":"The mucosal (inner) layer, the muscularis (middle, muscle) layer, and the serosal (outer) layer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Where does primary gallbladder cancer usually begin, and how does it spread?","answer":"Primary gallbladder cancer starts in the inner (mucosal) layer and spreads through the outer layers as it grows."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are two risk factors for developing gallbladder cancer?","answer":"Being female and being Native American are risk factors for gallbladder cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"What signs and symptoms might indicate gallbladder cancer?","answer":"Possible signs and symptoms include jaundice, fever, pain above the stomach, nausea and vomiting, bloating, and lumps in the abdomen."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000020_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Do most people with gallstones go on to develop gallbladder cancer?","answer":"No, patients with gallstones rarely develop gallbladder cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Having had an undescended testicle is a risk factor for testicular cancer.","source_span":"Having had an undescended testicle."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Risk factors for testicular cancer also include abnormal development of the testicles, a personal history of testicular cancer, a family history of testicular cancer (especially in a father or brother), and being white.","source_span":"Having had abnormal development of the testicles. - Having a personal history of testicular cancer. - Having a family history of testicular cancer (especially in a father or brother). - Being white."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Certain treatments for testicular cancer can cause infertility that may be permanent.","source_span":"Certain treatments for testicular cancer can cause infertility that may be permanent."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Patients who may wish to have children should consider sperm banking before having testicular cancer treatment.","source_span":"Patients who may wish to have children should consider sperm banking before having treatment."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"The three tumor markers used in staging testicular cancer are alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH).","source_span":"The following 3 tumor markers are used in staging testicular cancer: - Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) - Beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (-hCG). - Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Cancer can spread through three ways in the body: tissue, the lymph system, and the blood.","source_span":"Cancer can spread through tissue, the lymph system, and the blood"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"The stages used for testicular cancer are Stage 0 (Testicular Intraepithelial Neoplasia), Stage I, Stage II, and Stage III.","source_span":"The following stages are used for testicular cancer: - Stage 0 (Testicular Intraepithelial Neoplasia) - Stage I - Stage II - Stage III"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"In Stage 0 testicular cancer, abnormal cells are found in the tubules where sperm cells begin to develop, and all tumor marker levels are normal.","source_span":"In stage 0, abnormal cells are found in the tiny tubules where the sperm cells begin to develop... All tumor marker levels are normal."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Stage I testicular cancer is divided into stage IA, stage IB, and stage IS, and is determined after an inguinal orchiectomy is done.","source_span":"Stage I is divided into stage IA, stage IB, and stage IS and is determined after an inguinal orchiectomy is done."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","statement":"Tumor marker levels are measured again after inguinal orchiectomy and biopsy in order to determine the stage of testicular cancer.","source_span":"Tumor marker levels are measured again, after inguinal orchiectomy and biopsy, in order to determine the stage of the cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What are the risk factors for testicular cancer?","answer":"Risk factors include having had an undescended testicle, abnormal development of the testicles, a personal history of testicular cancer, a family history of testicular cancer (especially in a father or brother), and being white."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"Why should testicular cancer patients who want children in the future consider sperm banking before treatment?","answer":"Certain testicular cancer treatments can cause permanent infertility, so patients who may wish to have children should consider sperm banking before treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which three tumor markers are used in staging testicular cancer?","answer":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Through what three routes can cancer spread within the body?","answer":"Cancer can spread through tissue (growing into nearby areas), through the lymph system, and through the blood."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the main stages used to classify testicular cancer?","answer":"The stages are Stage 0 (Testicular Intraepithelial Neoplasia), Stage I, Stage II, and Stage III."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000021_1","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"What characterizes Stage 0 testicular cancer, and how is Stage I further subdivided?","answer":"In Stage 0, abnormal cells are found in the tubules where sperm cells begin to develop and all tumor marker levels are normal; Stage I is divided into stages IA, IB, and IS, determined after an inguinal orchiectomy."}
